This performance test came from a 2012 jbuilder pull request by rolftimmermans,
"Substantial performance improvements", where it was used to demonstrate
speedups to jbuilder's internals:

  https://github.com/rails/jbuilder/pull/54

How this differs from the original
----------------------------------

The original was a pure rendering benchmark with no caching: an article with an
author, 100 references and 100 comments, rebuilt on every iteration. The 2017
port extended it into a cross-library comparison (turbostreamer with its oj and
wankel encoders, jbuilder and props_template, through the analyzer harness).

Current differences from the original script:

- The article is now a cached fragment with live values (generated_at,
  request_id, total_comments) rendered around it, and the suite runs both with
  caching off (the original's pure-builder comparison) and on (fragment reuse).
  A response that is cacheable end to end would be cached above the view layer
  instead -- conditional GET, a CDN, or a low-level cache around the payload --
  so the cached mode models the realistic case: a cached fragment with live
  data around it.
- Caching is toggled by PERFORM_CACHING (see lib.rb and the Rakefile); each
  library reads it from the render context it is given.
- All implementations render through the view-layer template classes and emit a
  byte-identical document (dates are a single pre-formatted string), cache the
  same fragment under the same key, and do no file I/O in the measured loop.
